{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "87",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 20s presented to the emergency department with 1 day of chest pressure. Four days prior to presentation he experienced subjective fevers, rhinorrhea, a dry cough, and sinus congestion. The day prior to presentation he experienced 3 hours of substernal pressure that resolved spontaneously. In the early morning of the day of presentation, he was awakened from sleep by constant chest pressure radiating to the right jaw, diaphoresis, and 1 episode of vomiting. He reported no medical history.\n\nVital signs obtained in the Emergency Department were within normal limits. Cardiac examination revealed a regular rate and rhythm, normal point of maximum impulse without lifts, and no murmurs, rubs, or gallops in supine, sitting, or standing positions. The remainder of the physical examination was within normal limits.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\n  * Initial serum troponin I level: 6.18 ng/mL\n  * Elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein (values increased over next 3 days)\n\n- Imaging and Other Tests:\n  * ECG Findings: Normal sinus rhythm, ST-segment elevations with PR-segment depression in leads II, III, and aVF, ST-segment depression in lead V1, and PR-segment elevation in lead aVR\n  * Coronary angiography: Normal coronary arteries, left ventricular end-diastolic pressure 12 mm Hg (reference range, 4-12 mm Hg)\n  * Transthoracic echocardiogram: Normal results",
    "root": {
      "label": "Acute Chest Pain Syndrome with Suspected Cardiac or Inflammatory Aetiology and Uncharacterised ECG Abnormality",
